---
// The plain, non-hydrated header every signed-in page of this product renders (PK-4) —
// the wordmark and links to the three sections a signed-in visitor moves between: their
// packs, their closet, and their account.
//
// IT IS NO LONGER THE GEAR CLOSET'S NAV, WHICH IS WHY THE LABEL NO LONGER SAYS SO. PK-37
// added /packs and /packs/<id>, which render this same header, so a nav announcing itself
// as "Gear closet" would name one of the two sections it links to — and would tell a
// screen-reader user they were in the closet while they stood in the pack editor. The FILE
// keeps its name: renaming it is a rename of every import of it and buys nothing this
// comment does not.
//
// KEEP THIS A PLAIN .astro COMPONENT. It imports nothing from src/lib/auth/ (only the
// dependency-free path constants below, which is exactly what those modules exist
// for — see their own module comments), takes no `client:*` directive anywhere it is
// used, and must never gain one: a hydrated nav bar is a component the browser
// downloads, and the whole point of src/lib/gear/routes.ts, src/lib/packs/routes.ts and
// src/lib/auth-routes.ts being importable from anywhere is that a component like this one
// can render a link without ever becoming a reason for an auth SDK to reach the client.
// Neither GEAR_TRASH_PATH nor GEAR_NEW_PATH is imported any more, and each was dropped
// by a different ticket: PK-60 deleted the trash page outright, and PK-62 removed the
// "Add item" link from this nav. What is left is DESTINATIONS, which is what this header
// is for — see the note at the foot of the list about the action link that is not coming
// back.
import { GEAR_PATH } from '../lib/gear/routes';
// Dependency-free, on exactly the same footing as GEAR_PATH above. That module's header
// names two things it must stay importable BY — src/middleware.ts and PK-37's reorder
// island — and this component is neither of them; it is simply another beneficiary of the
// same property, which is the point of the property. A nav that can name a destination
// without acquiring a dependency is what keeps this file a plain .astro component.
import { PACKS_PATH } from '../lib/packs/routes';
import { ACCOUNT_PATH } from '../lib/auth-routes';
// The wordmark's destination, which used to be a hard-coded "/" and is the single click
// this whole change is about. Every page this nav renders on belongs to a signed-in
// visitor, and `/` now takes that visitor to their own closet instead of to a marketing
// page with a "Sign in" link on it. Named rather than inlined so the one place that
// decides what `/` means (src/lib/routes.ts) is reachable from here by reading.
import { HOME_PATH } from '../lib/routes';

// aria-current="page" on whichever link matches the current path — a small,
// server-computed affordance (no JS needed) that tells a screen reader, and a sighted
// visitor via :focus/underline styling, which section they are already in.
//
// PACKS SITS BETWEEN THE TWO EXISTING ENTRIES rather than at either end, which is a
// decision about what this header is a list of: the closet is where gear lives, packs are
// what gets built out of it, and the account is the settings drawer at the end. Appending
// Packs after Account would have put a section behind the settings; putting it first would
// have moved Closet under a signed-in visitor's cursor for no reason PK-37 has.
const path = Astro.url.pathname;
const links: readonly [href: string, label: string][] = [
  [GEAR_PATH, 'Closet'],
  [PACKS_PATH, 'Packs'],
  [ACCOUNT_PATH, 'Account'],
];
---

{
  /* THE STRIP (DESIGN.md §2.3) — a sheet with zero padding at a fixed height, lying on the
     paper like every other surface. This used to be a `border-b` header, which §13 refuses
     outright ("borders as separators"): in this language depth comes from the sheet's own
     shadow instead (§1). The 30px of top padding is the standard gap between papers.

     .paper-col rather than max-w-5xl: §3's content column is 1000px INCLUDING its 40px side
     padding, and every sheet on every page aligns to that same column. */
}
<div class="paper-col pt-[30px]">
  <header class="strip">
    {
      /* aria-label="Sections", not "Gear closet". A nav's accessible name is announced as the
       name of the LANDMARK — "Sections navigation" — so it has to describe what the list
       is, not which page rendered it. It is still labelled at all (rather than left as a
       bare <nav>) because a second navigation landmark already exists on some of these
       pages: the closet's pager, and the pack editor's picker pages, both of which carry
       their own aria-label for the same reason. */
    }
    <nav aria-label="Sections" class="flex h-full items-center justify-between px-6">
      {
        /* The wordmark is the SYSTEM voice — it is the app naming itself, not something the
           visitor wrote (§4). It used to be `font-mono`, and there is no monospace family in
           this design any more: the measurement that retired it is that Klee One's digits are
           uniform width natively, so nothing needed a mono face to make columns align. */
      }
      <a href={HOME_PATH} class="eyebrow font-medium tracking-[0.18em] no-underline">Packsheet</a>
      <ul class="flex items-center gap-6 text-sm">
        {
          links.map(([href, label]) => (
            <li>
              <a
                href={href}
                aria-current={path === href ? 'page' : undefined}
                class="text-ink-2 hover:text-ink aria-[current=page]:text-ink font-medium no-underline aria-[current=page]:underline"
              >
                {label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
        {
          /* PK-62 REMOVED THE "Add item" LINK FROM THIS NAV — read this before putting
           it back, because the C1 (PK-4 review) defect it was added for is NOT
           re-opened by removing it.

           C1 was that GEAR_NEW_PATH used to be linked ONLY from the closet's EMPTY
           STATE, so once the closet held a single item there was no route to it at all
           short of typing the URL. That defect stays fixed without this link: the same
           review also gave the closet list its own "Add item" button in its heading row,
           rendered whether the closet is empty or full (src/pages/gear/index.astro), and
           that button is the one PK-62 kept. On the closet list — where adding an item is
           actually the thing you are there to do — nothing changed but the removal of a
           second link to a route already linked three inches away.

           The cost PK-62 accepted was one extra click from the OTHER gear pages. That
           cost then shrank on its own: PK-60 deleted the trash page, so the only page
           left that renders this nav without its own Add item button is an item's detail
           page. If "add from anywhere" turns out to matter more than keeping this header
           to destinations only, the fix is a considered one (a persistent action
           affordance), not quietly restoring this <li>. */
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </header>
</div>
